= Confirmation bias — Technical =

== Positive test strategy ==
Suppose hypothesis H predicts property P.

A reasoner may preferentially test cases expected to have P.

This can be efficient when P is rare and diagnostic, but misleading when alternative hypotheses predict P equally well.

Thus positive testing and confirmation bias are related but not identical.

== Bayesian contrast ==
Updating should depend on likelihood ratios:

<pre>
posterior odds
= prior odds × P(E|H) / P(E|¬H)
</pre>

Confirmation bias can arise when evidence generation, likelihood assessment, or alternative-hypothesis representation is asymmetric.

== Motivated reasoning ==
Directional goals can amplify confirmatory search or interpretation.

However, confirmation bias also appears in tasks without strong identity or motivational stakes.

== Research design ==
Blinding, preregistration, counterfactual hypotheses, diagnostic tests, and adversarial collaboration reduce specific confirmation pathways.

